```
--------------------The code you provided is a part of a test method for an ArrayList object, which I assume is a class or structure that behaves similar to a ArrayList in other languages. The method is checking if a specific index in the array contains a value, and it checks for the condition in the comment "// @@@ the array contains New_Ret". 

In C#, the equivalent Assert statement could be:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_containsNewRet == true);
```

This statement will cause an exception to be thrown if the condition is not met. 

This assumes that the test method is in a context where you have access to the Debug class and the Debug.Assert method, which is a feature of Windows Presentation Foundation (WPF) applications, not standard C# applications.

If you are using the `System.Diagnostics.Debug` class in a non-WPF project, you will need to configure your project to output assert statements to the output window. To do this, you can set the `Define DEBUG constant` in the `Build` tab of your project settings. After that, the `Debug.Assert` statements will be removed in the compiled code, but they will still be output to the output window and can be viewed when running the program. 

If you want to use `Debug.Assert` in your code, you can make it like this:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_containsNewRet, "The array does not contain the returned value");
```

This statement will output the message "The array does not contain the returned value" if the condition is not met.
